<u>17-SEP-2005 10:14 PM (NZ Time)</u><br>
What a day today would end up being.  Up at 2 AM, M and I took a quick shower to wake up and finished the last little bit of our packing.  That damn loud music was playing all night long, but I was surprised it didn't wake is up.  As you can imagine, it was cold and dark when we checked out of the hostel.  Hauling our luggage down the back alley, we passed the source of the loud music.  It was a club that came out of nowhere.  The building was completely unmarked. It must have been one of those secret clubs that you only know about from word of mouth.  I'm sure we got some interesting looks as we hauled our stuff pass the drunk people standing outside.  A few blocks later, we were at the bus terminal waiting 30 min for the bus to arrive.  I didn't think anyone else would be catching the bus this early but to my surprise, a lot of people started to show up.
<p>
The first problem of many that day would arrive.  I wasn't sure if we were suppose to get off at the international terminal or the domestic terminal.  You see, we had to ultimately get to New Zealand today which is an international destination but we also had to fly through Sydney first and change plans.  Nothing on my tickets or the itinerary said which terminal to get off at, so I assumed it was the international terminal.  The thing that would save us is that we got there with 2 hrs to spare.  And believe me, we would need all of those 2 hours.  We got off at the international terminal trying to figure out where the Qantas ticket counter was so we could check in our baggage.  For some reason, this was quite difficult to find and we ended up right in front of the international gate which was not open yet.  Looking at the departures screen, I didn't see our flight number listed but I had figured, since the terminal wasn't open yet, the screen was updated.  Though after 15 min, M started to worry so we headed back and finally found the Qantas ticket counter.  After just a brief wait, we found out that we were suppose to be at the domestic terminal for our flight.  Ack!  M and I hurried for the domestic terminal as we had wasted 30 min already.  When we got there, there was a huge line in front of us. Go figure.  But we finally got through the line and checked our baggage in.
<p>
The next problem of the day came at security.  You see, M and bought a letter opener back in Sydney and we threw it in my carryon bag and forgot about it completely.  Unfortunately, the security scanners didn't forget it.  We were stopped and pulled aside as they asked us if we had a letter opener in my bag.  That's when it hit me that we did put it in there.  Of course, this is a bad time to realize it because the security officer gave us only 2 choices. He would have to confiscate the letter opener or we would have to check it in with our handcarry bag which contained fragile items.  M tried to argue with him over and over again about how it's just a present, but he kept repeating his 2 choices.  We had only 40 min left and I knew they were start boarding the plane soon, so I finally told him to just toss it.  M was disappointed but accepted that as our only option.  Finally, we got through security and made our plane just as they started boarding.  
<p> 
We finally left Melbourne and arrived in Sydney where we now had to get out of the plane, go through Customs and Immigration and then board the same exact plane we just left.  How dumb.  You'd think they would done this in Melbourne instead by calling it an international flight there.  Qantas really needs to take a look at how they handle this flight.
<p>
Aboard the same plane again, we seemed to sit there for an eternity.  I had looked at the watch a wondered why we hadn't taken off yet when the captain finally came over the intercom and said that they were having some warning lights come on and had to call in some engineers to look into it so the flight would be slightly delayed.  An hour went by and the people on the plane were getting restless.  The captain hadn't given an update and we were all wondering what was going on.  
<p>
Finally, the captain came on the intercom and explained to everyone that the fuel injection line was not working properly and as a safety precaution, we would all have to disembark the plane.  You could literally hear one loud (SIGH) of disappointment from everyone.  We were bussed back to the terminal where we sat around and waited, once again with no update on what was going to happen next.  To Qantas's credit, I'm glad we were sitting in the terminal rather than the plane itself.  
<p>
Finally, after several people started complaining, one of the reps from Qantas told us that they were fixing the plane and it wouldn't be ready for another hour but we could all get a snack to eat via a $5 food voucher they were handing out at the Qantas customer service desk.  
<p>
You can imagine the horde of people getting up and mobbing the customer service desk for their free food voucher.  Of course, this being an airport, you could hardly get a bottle of water for $5, but with M and my voucher, we had $10 worth of food and decided to split some food.  We only managed to go over by 50 cents which I still happen to have leftover.  We finally headed back to the gate and when the time came, we boarded the busses again to be taken back out to the plane.  Shortly after, we left off the ground and towards New Zealand, only 4 hrs later than expected.
<p>
New Zealand runs on a specific schedule.  Everything shuts down at 5 PM, I mean everything.  And of course our plane got there at 5 PM and I was so worried about getting our rental van in time.  We arrived to cold,wet, and cloudy weather in Queenstown.  Getting through immigration was quite easy but as we went to get our baggage we were met by the sniffer beagle dog.  It's job was to smell for fruits,vegetables,drugs,etc. in everyone's baggage.  However, when it got to us, it started hanging around M's purse.  Now we don't normally carry fruit with us but on the plan an apple was given to us and M couldn't eat it so she put it in her purse and forgot about it.  Obviously, the dog was well trained and it found the apple which was confiscated.  Of course this little stop caused us to be the very last people to go through customs.  M was a little worried after the dog incident that she dumped some packs of jelly she had grabbed from the plane into the trash can.  This time we had no issues with customs and got through quickly whereas others were getting further searched.
<p>
M had to seriously go to the bathroom but I wanted to pick up the rental van because we would be sleeping in it tonight and as I said earlier I was worried about things being closed.  As I got there to the rental desk, they were expecting me and I filled out the paperwork and paid a $50 fee for one way transportation of the van.  They were rudely hurrying us along as they wanted to get out of there because our late plane made them work overtime. I told them we needed just 10-15 min more so that M could go to the bathroom, but they were stubborn and stern with me and told me that they weren't even suppose to be there and if I didn't pick up the van right now, they would close the office.  It was almost a threat. I was a little ticked off (don't worry I filed a complaint with their headquarters later) and told M to go to the bathroom as I took the luggage outside.  We were greeted by another employee of the rental van company who showed us the specifics of the van including hastily and then left.  The van was huge, I hadn't driven such a big vehicle in such a long time. Of course it was a manual transmission which isn't a problem for me once I get adjusted to it, but I had killed it twice in the beginning just out of the parking lot of the airport.
<p>
With time against us as the darkness was falling, I had no clue where to stay and we had no money converted over yet. We were going to do it at the airport had the Travelex office not shut down already.  We drove into downtown Queenstown, which reminded me of a small alpine village, and came across a money conversion office, but of course there was no parking available.
<p>
Still not comfortable with the big van, I had underestimated it's width and actually cliped a bus with my side mirror.  Nothing was damaged on either vehicle, only the mirror was pushed inward and would need to be adjusted but it still nonetheless freaked me out.  I finally pulled over into a park and calmed down for a minute.  I turned around out of the park and headed back towards town where I find some parking just outside of town and we walked in together to the money conversion office.
<p>
After we got enough money to last us most of the trip, we were starving so we had dinner at one of the few places still open which was of course a Subway.
<p>
The only other thing we needed to do was to stock up on groceries since we would be heading out of Queenstown tomorrow morning.  We found out that one grocery store was still open until 9 PM so we had about 45 min to get there.  We walked back to the van and drove over to the grocery store and stocked up on supplies before we finally headed back to where we had originally parked. That's right we were going to be staying on the street itself. 
<p>
Tonight would be the first night we free camp.  That meant we had no electricity, no heat, and only the water which was filled in our freshwater tank.  It had been quite a long day and we hit the sack immediately.  Not wanting to gain any unnecessary attention, we closed up all the curtains and turned off the lights quickly.

